8. Accessibility acceptance criteria
Perform a contrast review for every combination of foreground/background after the migration:
- Main headings and body copy.
- Secondary text and helper text.
- Links in default, hover, focus and visited states if visited styling exists.
- Primary and secondary buttons in all states.
- Disabled controls.
- Input labels, placeholder text, borders, selected and focus states.
- Navigation links, active links, dropdown items and menu controls.
- Text over imagery and dark surfaces.
- Status banners and inline error/success messages.
- Icon-only controls and their boundaries.
- Modal text, close controls and backdrop.
Rules:
- Text contrast must meet WCAG AA: 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text.
- UI component boundary/icon contrast must meet 3:1 where required.
- Never rely only on colour to indicate success, error, warning, selected or active state; preserve existing textual/icon cues.
- Maintain visible focus indicators. The exact focus colour may be changed, but do not remove :focus-visible styling or its outline offset.
- Check text over image overlays at desktop and mobile widths; do not change overlay geometry just to hide weak contrast. Choose an approved foreground/background token combination.
- Do not use muted gray #ACADA8 for essential small text.
- Do not use white text on light olive.
If the current site already has an accessibility issue unrelated to colour, report it separately rather than changing layout or semantics in this task.
